    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with the specificity required for dermatological coding in our new billing cycle. We're seeing a lot of cases involving atypical presentations of cutaneous lesions—specifically those with irregular borders, heterogeneous pigmentation, and unusual textural changes that don't clearly fall under standard melanocytic nevus categories. Can you walk me through the hierarchy for selecting the most specific ICD-10 code when the morphology is ambiguous or presents with atypical features?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When coding atypical skin lesions, it's essential to start with the broadest category. Begin by assigning ICD-10 code L71 for \"Benign neoplasms of skin, suspected benign neoplasm of skin, or melanitical nevus.\" Then, narrow down the code to L73 for \"Benign melanocytic neoplasms\" or L74 for \"Otherwise unspecified benign melanocytic neoplasms.\" If the lesion still doesn't fit, move down to L75 for \"Distinguish melanocytic, mast cell, or follicular lesions\" or L76 for \"Other racially predominant benign neoplasms of skin.\" Document a detailed description of the lesion's morphology to support the most specific code available.",
